CHAPTER 2: THE ECONOMICS OF INVENTIVE ABUNDANCE

The patent system imposes artificial scarcity on non-rival knowledge goods to internalise the positive externality of invention. The classical welfare trade-off requires that monopoly incentives minus deadweight loss be positive. This trade-off unravels under AI abundance, a phenomenon we term the AI Invention Jevons Paradox: as AI reduces the marginal cost of generating a candidate invention toward zero, total candidate volume explodes, not contracts. When many AI-generated “inventions” are trivially obvious to an AI yet novel under a strained examination system, the system produces vast deadweight loss without commensurate innovation incentive.

Formally, let $I_{AI}(t)$ be the rate of AI-generated inventions. As $I_{AI}(t) \to \infty$ but the rate of genuinely high-social-value inventions grows sub-linearly, total deadweight loss $\sum_i DWL_i$ overwhelms total incentive rents, turning the patent system welfare-negative. Concentration risk magnifies this: large firms can weaponise AI to generate dense patent thickets that exclude competitors. AI can thus exacerbate “superstar” firm dominance, offsetting productivity gains with rising market power. The AIPI’s economic architecture directly addresses this by (a) calibrating protection terms to prevent pure rent accumulation, (b) instituting anti-thicket measures like compulsory licensing for non-use, and (c) offering an Innovation Commons that channels invention into the public domain with economic rewards that rival exclusivity.

