CHAPTER 4: THE GLOBAL KNOWLEDGE GRAPH: ARCHITECTURE AND ENRICHED NOVELTY INDEX

The Global Knowledge Graph (GKG) remains the operational cornerstone, but its novelty function is fundamentally strengthened to avoid the pitfall of equating novelty with pure semantic distance.

Definition 4.1 (GKG). A directed weighted hypergraph $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}, \phi, \psi)$ where $\phi$ maps knowledge nodes to dense multimodal embeddings, and $\psi$ weights relationships including taxonomic, causal, and functional links. The GKG ingests patents, scientific literature, technical standards, open-source code, and Innovation Commons registrations, with cryptographic audit trails to prevent tampering.

Original flaw: The simple $NI(I) = 1 - e^{-\alpha \Delta(I)}$, where $\Delta(I)$ is the minimum Euclidean distance to any prior art embedding, fails to capture the legal nuance that a semantically close invention can be novel if it performs a new function, and a distant one can be obvious if it is a trivial extrapolation.

Restated Enriched Novelty Index ($NI_+$):

We define $NI_+(I) \in [0,1]$ as:

NI_+(I) = \Phi \big( w_1 \cdot f_{dist}(\Delta(I)) + w_2 \cdot f_{func}(I) + w_3 \cdot f_{causal}(I) \big)

Where:

· $f_{dist}$: A calibrated sigmoid of the GKG semantic distance, preserving the vector-space contribution.
· $f_{func}$: Functional novelty—computed by comparing the causal-functional subgraph of the claimed invention to all known functional primitives in the GKG. If the function (the problem-solution linkage) is entirely absent, $f_{func} \to 1$. If the same function is already achieved by a different mechanism, $f_{func}$ is moderate.
· $f_{causal}$: Causal novelty—evaluates whether the invention relies on a fundamentally new physical, chemical, or logical mechanism, or a trivial substitution (e.g., “use a neural network” instead of a PID controller). It uses a domain-specific causal graph, updated continuously, and penalises mere algorithmic substitution.
· $w_1, w_2, w_3$: Sector-specific weights (e.g., in chemistry, functional identity matters more; in software, causal novelty is paramount).
· $\Phi$ normalises the weighted sum to $[0,1]$.

This enriched index is not a single pass/fail threshold but produces a vector $(f_{dist}, f_{func}, f_{causal})$ presented to the examiner. Cases where semantic distance is high but functional novelty is low (obvious substitution) are automatically flagged.

Proposition 4.1 (Adversarial Completeness). The GKG satisfies prior art completeness only if it maintains a cryptographic Merkle tree of all ingested documents and allows any third party to challenge completeness. Suspiciously timed “prior art” injections (synthetic prior art) are detected via temporal anomaly analysis and quarantined (Chapter 10).

The GKG’s multimodal extension integrates chemical graphs, engineering drawings, and biological sequences, each contributing to the embedding. Temporal decay weights ensure that knowledge ages appropriately, preventing ancient, obsolete concepts from unfairly blocking novelty.

