CHAPTER 5: MULTI-DIMENSIONAL EVALUATION PROTOCOL (MDEP)

The MDEP produces a five-index vector $(NI_+, UI, RI, II, RSI)$. We refine each index with stronger formalism:

Utility Index (UI): $UI(I) = \frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{d \in D} \text{Relevance}(I,d) \cdot \text{Impact}(I,d)$, where $D$ is the set of application domains identified by the GKG’s knowledge graph. For pharmaceuticals, UI integrates therapeutic benefit and disease burden; for software, deployment cost reduction and accessibility.

Reproducibility Index (RI): $RI(I) = P(\text{independent reproduction} \mid \text{specification})$. Estimated via structural completeness checks, claim-specification consistency, and, for high-impact applications, pre-grant independent replication. RI below threshold $\tau_{RI}$ mandates specification improvement.

Impact Index (II): $II(I) = w_1 \hat{C}(I) + w_2 \hat{M}(I) + w_3 \hat{B}(I)$ with explicit uncertainty bounds $\sigma_{II}$. Forward citation centrality, market estimates, and social benefit projections are drawn from the GKG’s temporal link prediction models.

Risk Index (RSI): $RSI(I) = 1 - \max_{r \in R} P(harm_r \mid I)$ for environmental, security, ethical, and systemic risk categories. Low RSI triggers extended review but not automatic rejection; it feeds into the Public Benefit Test (Chapter 9).

Theorem 5.1 (Non-Automability). No computable function $f: [0,1]^5 \to \{grant, deny\}$ is equivalent in welfare to human adjudication, because legal decisions depend on non-computable contextual factors (precedent, strategic intent, equity). Thus the MDEP is an advisory tool; final decisions rest with human examiners.

Human examiners receive an MDEP dashboard, with each index traced back to the underlying GKG evidence. They may depart from computational recommendations with documented reasoning, forming an auditable human-in-the-loop system.

