CHAPTER 7: ADAPTIVE PROTECTION CALCULUS (APC)

The APC derives sector-specific patent duration $T_s^*$ from welfare maximisation, replacing the politically fixed 20-year term.

Welfare function:
Maximise $W_s(T) = B_s(T) - DWL_s(T) - C_{enforcement}(T)$ subject to $W_s(T) \ge W_{baseline}$.

First-order condition yields:
T_s^* = \frac{2 R_s}{\rho_s P_s D_s}

Where $R_s$ is the R&D cost parameter, $\rho_s$ the discount rate, $P_s$ the profit margin premium, and $D_s$ the innovation velocity (rate of obsolescence). Sector calibrations:

· Pharmaceuticals: $R_s$ high ($2.5B), $D_s$ moderate, yielding $T^*_{pharma} \approx 20$–25 years from grant, incorporating regulatory review adjustment.
· Software & AI algorithms: $R_s$ extremely low with AI tools, $D_s$ very high (2–5 years), yielding $T^*_{software} \approx 3$–7 years. For purely AI-generated software, $T^* \to 0$ as $R_s \to 0$.
· Semiconductors: Intermediate, $T^* \approx 10$–15 years.
· Biotechnology: Platform technologies may merit longer terms; application-specific tools shorter.

Empirical calibration unit: A permanent Sectoral Innovation Monitoring Unit re-estimates $D_s$ and $R_s$ every five years from actual market data, patent lifecycle analysis, and R&D surveys. Patents granted under a prior term retain it; new grants reflect updated terms. This prevents political manipulation and ensures empirical grounding.

Adversarial robustness: The Unit publishes all data and models openly. Attempts to manipulate reported innovation velocity are countered by cross-validation with independent observatories and academic consortiums.

