CHAPTER 9: THE PUBLIC BENEFIT TEST: TIERED APPLICATION AND CONSTITUTIONAL LIMITS

The original PBT, applied universally, would be politically explosive. The restated PBT is tiered and constitutionally bounded.

Applicability: The full PBT with market concentration penalty applies only to Tier III inventions (AI-dominant, $\lambda_v \in [0.1, 0.4)$) and to any application that triggers a high-risk alert ($RSI < \tau_{risk}$). Tier I and Tier II inventions are exempt from the MC penalty but still subject to a minimal net-benefit threshold ($UI - RSI_{normalized} \ge \tau_{min}$) to weed out purely harmful inventions, consistent with existing ordre public exceptions.

Definition 9.1 (Tier III PBT). An invention $I$ in Tier III passes if:
PBT_{III}(I) = UI(I) - (1 - RSI(I)) - MC(I) \ge \tau_{PBT}


where $MC(I) = HHI_{post} - HHI_{pre}$, the increase in Herfindahl-Hirschman Index in the relevant technology market. If the patent would substantially concentrate an already concentrated market, it must demonstrate higher utility.

Constitutional limits: The PBT is a minimum threshold, not a full welfare balancing test. It does not authorise the patent office to refuse patents on broad “public interest” grounds outside demonstrable risk and market power. Its statutory basis is carefully circumscribed to avoid challenges under TRIPS and national constitutions. It acts as a safeguard, not a tool for industrial policy.

Remediation: A failing PBT does not mean automatic rejection. The applicant may:

· Commit to FRAND licensing.
· Dedicate certain claims to the Innovation Commons.
· Accept monitoring and compulsory licensing if not commercialised within a set period.

This calibrated approach makes the PBT politically viable while targeting the core AI-abundance risks.

