CHAPTER 10: ADVERSARIAL BEHAVIOR AND INSTITUTIONAL CAPTURE

This chapter is the critical addition that transforms AIPI from an idealised design into a resilient institution. We model five classes of adversarial threats and propose corresponding defences.

10.1 Patent Flooding
Large entities could use AI to generate millions of low-quality applications to overwhelm the system, forcing grant of trivial patents.
Defence: Dynamic examination fees that scale exponentially with application volume per entity per year, and with AI contribution fraction. Automated triage using the enriched NI₊ and MDEP indices immediately rejects applications falling below obviousness thresholds without human review, subject to appeal.

10.2 Embedding Manipulation
Applicants could craft claims to artificially inflate semantic distance from prior art (e.g., by inserting obscure jargon or rephrasing functions).
Defence: The enriched NI₊ uses functional and causal graphs that are resistant to linguistic surface variations. Adversarial training of the embedding model includes “patent-gaming” examples. The system also computes a gaming detection score based on syntactic anomaly and deviation from typical claim language, flagging suspicious cases for enhanced human review.

10.3 Synthetic Prior Art
Malicious actors could inject fabricated technical disclosures into the GKG corpus to poison novelty searches, either to block competitors or to create false “prior art” that invalidates future patents.
Defence: The GKG implements a cryptographic provenance chain for every document. New sources are validated against registries of known legitimate venues. A machine-learning anomaly detector flags sudden influxes of documents from unknown sources or with statistically unusual content patterns. Suspect documents are quarantined and do not affect NI₊ until verified by a human panel. Additionally, a “poison pill” rule automatically expels any source found to contain fabricated disclosures and blacklists its origin.

10.4 Knowledge Graph Attacks
Adversaries could attempt to manipulate the graph’s edge weights through coordinated citation rings or by funding biased research.
Defence: Edge weights incorporate a trust decay factor; citations from low-reliability sources are down-weighted. The system uses a variant of PageRank with reset to known authoritative nodes. Community-based anomaly detection identifies citation cartels.

10.5 Monopoly Capture of Governance
Large firms could dominate the Policy Council or MDEP calibration committees to tilt the system in their favour.
Defence: Governance mandates multi-stakeholder representation with rotating seats, strict conflict-of-interest rules, and supermajority requirements for parameter changes. All parameter changes (e.g., $\tau_{PBT}$, APC weights) are subject to a mandatory public comment period and an adversarial review panel composed of independent academics and civil-society representatives. The Transparency Mandate requires full publication of all committee deliberations.

10.6 Adversarial Stress Testing
Before full deployment, the AIPI must undergo red-team exercises: independent security researchers, patent attorneys, and economists attempt to break the system, identify loopholes, and measure its resilience. Results are made public, and the design is iterated accordingly. This continuous adversarial auditing becomes a permanent institutional function.

