CHAPTER 11: GOVERNANCE ARCHITECTURE AND HUMAN SOVEREIGNTY

The AIPI’s five-layer architecture remains, but is reinforced with adversarial safeguards and a constitutional grounding.

· Layer 1 – Technical Infrastructure (AI-Operated): Immutable GKG, MDEP computation engines, automated triage. All operations are logged on a public append-only transparency ledger.
· Layer 2 – Examination Panel (Human-AI Hybrid): A human examiner reviews the MDEP dashboard, enriched NI₊ vector, and VCSD audit. The examiner has full discretion, with documented reasoning, and can call for adversarial review if anomalies are flagged.
· Layer 3 – Review Board (Human-Majority): Appeals are heard by three expert humans, with AI-generated briefs but no voting weight.
· Layer 4 – Policy Council (Human-Exclusively): Composed of representatives from member states, technology sectors, civil society, and academia. Sets all parameters, subject to the adversarial governance rules of Chapter 10. No AI governance authority.
· Layer 5 – Judicial Review: All AIPI decisions are subject to national court review; the AIPI’s computational outputs are expert evidence, not binding determinations.

Theorem 11.1 (Constitutional Sovereignty). The AIPI’s governance is legitimate only if all final normative decisions—patent grants, term adjustments, PBT rulings—are made by humans accountable to democratic legal systems. AI serves as an evidence-generating instrument, not an authority. This requirement is derived from principles of administrative law and the rule of law.

The Transparency Mandate requires public auditability of all GKG embeddings and computations, disclosure of AI models, and annual third-party audits for bias. This eliminates opacity as a legitimacy defect.

Interoperability architecture: AIPI operates as a coordination mechanism, not a supranational body. National offices become Participating Offices (POs) by adopting GKG and MDEP protocols. The APC and Commons mechanisms are implemented domestically, respecting sovereignty.

