CHAPTER 13: CONCLUSION — FROM MONOPOLY RENT TO CIVILIZATIONAL PROGRESS

The patent system’s original justification—temporary monopoly in exchange for disclosure—made sense when invention was rare. AI has made invention abundant, transforming that bargain into a mechanism for rent accumulation. The AIPI, restated and fortified, offers a path to align intellectual property governance with technological reality. By enriching novelty assessment, verifying human contribution, adapting protection terms, creating genuine economic alternatives to patenting, and embedding adversarial resilience, it ensures that the inventive flood serves human flourishing, not monopolistic exclusion.

The ultimate metric is not administrative efficiency but whether the global IP system, reconstituted, accelerates beneficial technology deployment across all humanity—especially to those currently excluded by monopoly pricing. When the cost of invention tends to zero, the cost of access should follow. The AIPI is designed to make that principle institutionally real.



