APPENDIX B: VERIFIED CONTRIBUTION STRATIFICATION — ISL PROTOCOL AND AUDIT PROOF

B.1 Invention Session Ledger (ISL) Schema

ISL is an append-only, cryptographically signed ledger. Each entry in a session is a tuple:

```
Entry = {
    session_id: UUID,
    seq_num: integer,
    timestamp: ISO8601,
    actor: human_id | AI_system_id,
    action_type: prompt | generation | selection | validation | refinement,
    content_hash: SHA-256(content),
    metadata: { ... }
}
```

All entries are hashed and linked to previous entry (hash chain). The session ledger is sealed upon patent filing and signed by all parties.

B.2 Computation of Verified $\lambda_v$

An independent audit engine computes:

· $H_{conc}^{audit} = \sum_{entries \text{ of type } prompt} \text{complexity}(content) \times \text{refinement\_factor}$, where complexity is a function of structured problem description length and technical depth (scored by a standard NLP model).
· $H_{sel}^{audit} = \text{number of distinct selection actions weighted by downstream impact}$.
· $H_{val}^{audit} = \text{number of validation entries with confirmed experimental/computational verification}$.
· $A_{gen}^{audit} = \sum_{AI\_generation\_entries} \text{entropy\_generated} \times (1 - \text{human\_guidance\_score})$, where human_guidance_score is derived from preceding prompt specificity.
· $A_{opt}^{audit} = \sum_{AI\_optimization\_entries} \text{improvement\_magnitude}$.

The audit protocol applies these definitions to produce $\lambda_v$, which is compared to the applicant’s declared $\lambda_{decl}$. If $|\lambda_v - \lambda_{decl}| > \epsilon$ (e.g., 0.05), the application is rejected and the applicant fined.

B.3 Adversarial Resistance Proof (Sketch)

Claim: An applicant cannot arbitrarily inflate $\lambda_v$ without detection, assuming honest auditing of the ISL.

Proof sketch: The ISL records every interaction. Any attempt to fabricate human prompts (e.g., auto-generated text) will be detectable by anomaly detection on prompt entropy and stylistic consistency. The complexity scoring function is adversarially trained to discount repetitive or boilerplate prompts. The refinement factor requires evidence of non-trivial shifts in the invention concept, which can be verified by analyzing the sequence of generated output embeddings. Thus, an attacker who tries to simulate human contribution must either invest genuine human effort (which is the goal) or produce detectable machine-generated signals. ∎

