APPENDIX J: PROOF OF NON-AUTOMABILITY THEOREM

Theorem 5.1 (Non-Automability of Final Adjudication). No computable function $f: [0,1]^5 \to \{0,1\}$ (mapping MDEP vector to grant/deny) can be welfare-equivalent to human adjudication across all possible inventions, because there exist non-computable factors that legally and ethically must influence the decision.

Proof:
Assume, for contradiction, that such a function $f$ exists and is universally deployed. Consider an invention $I^*$ with MDEP vector $v = (0.9, 0.8, 0.7, 0.95, 0.1)$ (high novelty, utility, impact; low risk), but which is strategically filed by an entity known to be assembling a patent thicket to block a critical public-health technology. Under standard antitrust and equitable principles (e.g., the doctrine of patent misuse), a human tribunal may deny the patent or impose compulsory licensing regardless of the technical merits. This decision relies on the intent, context, and public interest—factors not encoded in $v$. No computable function can, from $v$ alone, distinguish this case from a benign application with identical MDEP profile filed by a research charity.

Further, by Rice’s Theorem, any non-trivial semantic property of a program (here, the “justice” of a patent decision) is undecidable in general when the decision must incorporate arbitrary external context. Since the legal system demands that such context be weighed, $f$ cannot be complete. ∎

This proof solidifies the principle of Human Sovereignty: AI provides indices, but final adjudication rests with humans accountable to law.

